---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import GuideMeta from '../../components/GuideMeta.astro';
import GuideReferences from '../../components/GuideReferences.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="HEIC vs JPG: Quality, Size, Metadata and Compatibility | Kit-Bin"
  description="What you actually gain and lose converting an iPhone photo from HEIC to JPG."
>

  <article class="max-w-2xl mx-auto px-4 pt-10 pb-16 text-ink dark:text-slate-300 space-y-4">
    <p class="text-sm"><a href="/guides/" class="text-primary dark:text-blue-400 hover:underline">← All guides</a></p>
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-ink dark:text-slate-100">HEIC vs JPG: A Real Comparison</h1>

    <p>
      HEIC is the newer format, JPG is the universal one, and converting between them trades
      compatibility against efficiency. Here's what actually changes on each axis.
    </p>

    <h2 class="tool-h2 font-semibold text-lg pt-2 text-ink dark:text-slate-100">Compression efficiency</h2>
    <p>
      HEIC uses the HEVC (H.265) codec's image compression, which is meaningfully more efficient
      than JPEG's decades-older algorithm. Apple advertises roughly half the file size at
      similar visual quality. This is real: a HEIC photo is typically much smaller than an
      equivalent-quality JPEG of the same picture.
    </p>

    <h2 class="tool-h2 font-semibold text-lg pt-2 text-ink dark:text-slate-100">File size after converting</h2>
    <p>
      Converting HEIC to JPG generally produces a larger file than the original HEIC, because
      you're moving from the more efficient codec to the less efficient one. This is expected and
      not a sign anything went wrong. You're trading file size for the ability to open the
      photo everywhere.
    </p>

    <h2 class="tool-h2 font-semibold text-lg pt-2 text-ink dark:text-slate-100">Visual quality</h2>
    <p>
      A well-done HEIC-to-JPG conversion at a reasonably high JPEG quality setting looks
      effectively identical to the original for normal viewing. You're re-encoding, which
      introduces some generational loss, but it's rarely visible unless you're pixel-peeping or
      the JPEG quality is set very low.
    </p>

    <h2 class="tool-h2 font-semibold text-lg pt-2 text-ink dark:text-slate-100">Metadata</h2>
    <p>
      Camera, location, and date metadata (EXIF data) doesn't automatically survive a format
      conversion the same reliable way it does when AirDropping or syncing through Apple's own
      ecosystem. How much metadata carries through depends on the specific converter. Don't
      assume location or timestamp data will definitely be preserved unless you've checked the
      output.
    </p>

    <h2 class="tool-h2 font-semibold text-lg pt-2 text-ink dark:text-slate-100">Compatibility</h2>
    <p>
      This is HEIC's actual weakness: it's an Apple-driven format, and support outside Apple's own
      devices and recent versions of Windows/Android is inconsistent. JPG opens correctly on
      literally every device, OS, and piece of software made in the last 25 years. If you're
      sharing a photo with someone whose setup you don't control, JPG is the safe choice.
    </p>

    <h2 class="tool-h2 font-semibold text-lg pt-2 text-ink dark:text-slate-100">When to actually convert</h2>
    <p>
      Keep HEIC if the photos stay within Apple devices and you value the smaller file size.
      Convert to JPG before sharing with Windows/Android users, uploading to a site that doesn't
      accept HEIC, or archiving somewhere long-term compatibility matters more than file size.
    </p>

    <p>
      <a href="/image/heic-to-jpg/" class="text-primary dark:text-blue-400 hover:underline">Convert HEIC to JPG</a>{' '}
      on this site runs entirely in your browser. See{' '}
      <a href="/guides/heic-explained/" class="text-primary dark:text-blue-400 hover:underline">why HEIC exists in the first place</a>{' '}
      for the fuller background.
    </p>
    <GuideReferences keys={['heif', 'mdnImageTypes']} />

    <GuideMeta
      headline={'HEIC vs JPG: Quality, Size, Metadata and Compatibility'}
      description={'A real technical comparison of HEIC and JPG, what you gain and lose converting an iPhone photo between the two.'}
      published="2026-08-04"
      reviewed="2026-08-05"
    />
  </article>
</Layout>
